use std
into pdf.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace pdf). It is the PDF object layer: it reads the file, finds every object through classic cross-reference tables, cross-reference streams and object streams (or by scanning the file when those are broken), parses objects into a node arena, decodes stream filters, sets up decryption, and collects the page list, document information, outline and form fields. Every module-level name starts with `pf_` or `PF_`. Parsing never trusts the file: every index is checked against its buffer, recursion depth is capped at 64 and loops over linked structures stop after 100000 steps.

```amni
import os;
import gfx.zlib;
import dm;
import dcrypt;
```

Node types are module constants: `PF_NULL = 0`, `PF_BOOL = 1`, `PF_INT = 2`, `PF_REAL = 3`, `PF_NAME = 4`, `PF_STR = 5`, `PF_ARR = 6`, `PF_DICT = 7`, `PF_REF = 8`, `PF_STREAM = 9`.

`struct PfNode` has `t int`, `a int`, `b int` and `r real`. For a bool a is 0 or 1; for an int a is the value; for a real r is the value; for a name or string a and b are the start and length of its decoded bytes in `pf_strs`; for an array a is its first slot in `pf_kids` and b the element count; for a dict a is its first slot in `pf_kids` and b the number of key and value pairs, stored key node then value node; for a ref a is the object number and b the generation; for a stream a is its dict node and b its index in `pf_streams`.

`struct PfStream` has `at int` and `len int` (the raw data in `pf_buf`), `num int` and `gen int` (the object it belongs to, num -1 inside object streams).

`struct PfXref` has `t int` (0 unknown or free, 1 at a file offset, 2 inside an object stream), `f2 int` (the offset or the object stream number) and `f3 int` (the generation or the index in the stream).

`struct PfPage` has `node int` (the page dict), `res int` (its resources dict after inheritance, -1 when none), `num int` (the page object number), `x0 real`, `y0 real`, `x1 real`, `y1 real` (the crop box, else the media box, default 0 0 612 792) and `rot int` (the rotation in degrees, a multiple of 90 from 0 to 270).

Module state: `var pf_buf byte[>..]` (the file), `var pf_nodes PfNode[>..]`, `var pf_kids int[>..]`, `var pf_strs byte[>..]`, `var pf_streams PfStream[>..]`, `var pf_xref PfXref[>..]` (indexed by object number), `var pf_cache int[>..]` (the node of each object number once parsed, -1 before), `var pf_busy bool[>..]` (objects being parsed, to stop reference cycles), `var pf_stack int[>..]`, `var pf_trailer = -1`, `var pf_root = -1`, `var pf_info = -1`, `var pf_pages PfPage[>..]`, `var pf_pageof int[>..]` (object number to page index plus 1, 0 when not a page), `var pf_cur_num = -1` and `var pf_cur_gen = 0` (the object being parsed, for string decryption; -1 means no decryption), `var pf_enc_num = -1` (the Encrypt dict's object number, never decrypted), `var pf_rebuilt = false`, `var pf_tmp byte[>..]`, `var pf_tmp2 byte[>..]`, `var pf_depth = 0`.

`pf_reset()` clears every buffer and counter above and puts a `PF_NULL` node at index 0 so that node 0 means null.

`pf_ws(c byte) bool` is true for the PDF whitespace bytes 0, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 32. `pf_delim(c byte) bool` is true for ( ) < > [ ] { } / and %. `pf_regular(c byte) bool` is neither whitespace nor a delimiter.

`pf_skip(s const byte[:], at int&)` advances at past whitespace and comments (a % up to the next carriage return or newline).

`pf_new(t int, a int, b int, r real) int` pushes a node and returns its index.

`pf_add_bytes(d const byte[:]) int` appends d to `pf_strs` and returns its start.

`pf_hexval(c byte) int` is the value of a hex digit or -1.

`pf_lit_string(s const byte[:], at int&, out byte[>..]&)` reads a literal string whose opening parenthesis is at at, appending its bytes to out and leaving at after the matching close: balanced nested parentheses are kept; backslash escapes n r t b f ( ) and backslash itself map to their bytes; a backslash and one to three octal digits is that byte (modulo 256); a backslash before an end of line (CR, LF or CRLF) is dropped together with the end of line; a backslash before any other byte keeps that byte; a bare CR or CRLF inside the string becomes LF. An unterminated string ends at the end of the buffer.

`pf_hex_string(s const byte[:], at int&, out byte[>..]&)` reads a hex string whose < is at at up to the closing >, ignoring whitespace and any non-hex byte, and appending one byte per pair of digits; an odd final digit is followed by an implied 0.

`pf_name(s const byte[:], at int&, out byte[>..]&)` reads a name whose / is at at, appending the regular bytes after it and turning # followed by two hex digits into that byte.

`pf_number(s const byte[:], at int&) int` reads a number at at and returns a new `PF_INT` or `PF_REAL` node: an optional sign (+ or -, repeated signs are tolerated), digits and at most one period (a leading period is allowed); it is a real when it has a period, and an int otherwise (ints beyond 2^53 become reals).

`pf_parse(s const byte[:], at int&) int` parses one object at at (after `pf_skip`) and returns its node, or -1 at the end of the buffer or on a byte that starts no object (in which case at is advanced by one byte). With `pf_depth` above 64 return node 0. Cases: "<<" starts a dict: repeatedly skip, stop at ">>", parse a name key (a key that is not a name ends the dict), parse its value (a value of -1 ends the dict), push both on `pf_stack`; at the end copy the pushed pairs into `pf_kids`, cut the stack back and create the dict node; when a "stream" keyword follows the dict (after whitespace), read the stream with `pf_stream_body` and return a `PF_STREAM` node. "<" alone is a hex string; "(" a literal string; both are decrypted with `dcrypt::dc_decrypt` (as a string) when `pf_cur_num` is not -1 and not `pf_enc_num`; strings and names go to `pf_strs` through `pf_add_bytes`. "[" starts an array of objects up to "]", collected through `pf_stack` the same way. "/" is a name. A digit, sign or period is a number; when it is a non-negative int, look ahead without consuming: whitespace, another non-negative int, whitespace and the keyword R (followed by a delimiter, whitespace or the end) make a `PF_REF` of the two ints. The keywords true and false are bools, null is node 0. Any other keyword (a run of regular bytes) returns node 0 after consuming it. Increase `pf_depth` while parsing nested values and restore it on every return.

`pf_stream_body(s const byte[:], at int&, dict int) int` reads stream data after the dict: at points at the keyword "stream"; skip it and one end of line (CRLF, LF, or a lone CR); data starts there. Its length is the dict's /Length when it is an int or a ref to an int (resolved with `pf_get` after the dict is complete); when that length is missing, negative, runs past the buffer, or the bytes after the data (after optional whitespace) are not "endstream", find the next "endstream" from the data start instead and use the distance to it minus a trailing end of line. Leave at after "endstream". Push a `PfStream` with `pf_cur_num` and `pf_cur_gen` and return the new `PF_STREAM` node whose a is dict.

`pf_name_is(n int, v const byte[:]) bool` is true when node n (after `pf_deref`) is a name equal to v. `pf_bytes(n int) const byte[:]` is the bytes of a name or string node (empty for other nodes). `pf_int(n int, def int) int` is the value of an int node, or of a real node rounded toward zero, after `pf_deref`, else def. `pf_real(n int, def real) real` is the same for reals. `pf_len(n int) int` is the element count of an array node after `pf_deref`, else 0. `pf_at(n int, i int) int` is element i of an array after `pf_deref` and itself dereferenced, or -1. `pf_kind(n int) int` is the type constant (`PF_NULL` to `PF_STREAM`) of node n after `pf_deref`, or -1 when that is not a valid node.

`pf_dict_of(n int) int` is n for a dict, the stream's dict for a stream (both after `pf_deref`), else -1. `pf_dget(n int, key const byte[:]) int` looks key up in `pf_dict_of(n)` and returns the dereferenced value node, or -1 when absent or null.

`pf_deref(n int) int` follows refs: while n is a `PF_REF` node, n = `pf_get` of its object number (at most 32 steps, -1 when the object is missing).

`pf_get(num int) int` returns the node of indirect object num, parsing it on first use and caching it in `pf_cache` (grow `pf_cache` and `pf_busy` as needed). Return -1 for a number outside the xref, a free or unknown entry, or an object already being parsed. Type 1: parse at the offset with `pf_parse_indirect`; when that fails and the table was not rebuilt yet, call `pf_rebuild()` and try once more. Type 2: `pf_from_objstm(f2, f3, num)`.

`pf_parse_indirect(off int, num int) int` expects "num gen obj" at off (skipping whitespace; the number read must equal num unless num is -1), sets `pf_cur_num` and `pf_cur_gen` while parsing the object after it, restores them, and returns the object's node or -1.

`pf_from_objstm(snum int, idx int, num int) int` loads object idx of object stream snum: get the stream node with `pf_get`, decode it with `pf_decode` into a fresh buffer kept for that stream (cache decoded object streams in a module list so each is decoded once), read the 2 × /N header ints (object number and relative offset pairs), then parse the object at /First plus the offset of entry idx (when entry idx's object number differs from num, search the pairs for num instead) with `pf_cur_num` set to -1. Parsing reads from the decoded buffer, not `pf_buf`.

The cross-reference reader fills `pf_xref` (grown to the highest object number seen); an entry is only set when it is still unknown, so newer sections, which are read first, win.

`pf_set_xref(num int, t int, f2 int, f3 int)` grows `pf_xref` with unknown entries to cover num (refusing numbers above 10 million) and sets the entry when it is still unknown.

`pf_read_table(at int) int` reads a classic table at at (the keyword "xref"): subsections of a start number and a count followed by count entries of an offset, a generation and n or f, parsed as tokens (so entries of 19, 20 or 21 bytes all work); "n" entries become type 1 with the offset and generation, "f" entries mark the number as free (type 0 counts as set). A subsection whose start is 1 while its first entry is a free entry with generation 65535 is shifted to start at 0. After the keyword "trailer" parse the dict and return it (or -1).

`pf_read_xstream(node int)` reads a cross-reference stream: decode it with `pf_decode`; widths from /W (three ints); /Index pairs (default 0 and /Size); for each entry read the three fields as big-endian numbers of their widths (a zero first width means type 1); type 0 is free, 1 an offset and generation, 2 an object stream number and index.

`pf_read_xref(start int) bool` follows the chain from start: at each offset (skipping whitespace), "xref" means a classic table, whose trailer also may name /XRefStm (read that stream right after the table, at its offset with `pf_parse_indirect`); otherwise parse an indirect object there, which must be a stream with /Type /XRef, and its dict is the trailer. The first trailer read becomes `pf_trailer`. Continue with the trailer's /Prev offset, refusing an offset already visited and stopping after 64 sections. Return whether at least one section was read.

`pf_rebuild()` rebuilds the table by scanning the whole file: for every place where an unsigned int, whitespace, an unsigned int, whitespace and the keyword "obj" appear (the first int preceded by whitespace, a delimiter or the file start), overwrite the entry for that number with type 1 at the offset of the first int (later occurrences overwrite earlier ones). Clear `pf_cache`. Then look for a trailer: for each "trailer" keyword parse the dict after it and take the last one that has /Root; when none has, use any parsed object stream of /Type /XRef with /Root; when still none, search the parsed objects for a dict with /Type /Catalog and make a trailer dict node holding just /Root pointing to it (create the ref node and the name node "Root" in the arena). Object streams found during the scan (dicts with /Type /ObjStm) are expanded too: for each, record type 2 entries for the objects listed in its header unless the number already has a type 1 entry. Set `pf_rebuilt`.

`pf_png_unpredict(d byte[>..]&, columns int, colors int, bpc int)` undoes PNG row prediction in place: bytes per pixel is max(1, colors × bpc / 8), the row length colors × bpc × columns / 8 rounded up; each row starts with a filter type byte (0 none, 1 sub, 2 up, 3 average, 4 Paeth, anything else treated as none) which is dropped from the output.

`pf_tiff_unpredict(d byte[>..]&, columns int, colors int, bpc int)` undoes TIFF predictor 2 for 8 bits per component (each byte adds the byte colors positions earlier in the same row); other bit depths are left as they are.

`pf_lzw(src const byte[:], early int, out byte[>..]&)` decodes LZW data with codes of 9 to 12 bits read most significant bit first, a clear code 256, an end code 257 and the code width growing one code early when early is 1 (the PDF default).

`pf_a85(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` decodes ASCII base-85: ignore whitespace, stop at "~>" , "z" is four zero bytes, a final partial group of n characters is padded with "u" and yields n minus 1 bytes. `pf_ahex(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` decodes hex digits up to ">" ignoring other bytes. `pf_rle(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` decodes RunLengthDecode (a length byte below 128 copies the next length plus 1 bytes, above 128 repeats the next byte 257 minus length times, 128 ends).

`pf_decode(n int, out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and fills it with the decoded data of stream node n (after `pf_deref`; false when not a stream). Start from the raw bytes; decrypt them with `dcrypt::dc_decrypt` (as a stream, with the stream's object number and generation) when encryption is on, the object number is not -1, the stream's dict is not /Type /XRef, and its filter list does not contain /Crypt. Then apply each filter of /Filter (a name or an array of names) in order, with the matching /DecodeParms entry (a dict, or an array of dicts and nulls): FlateDecode or Fl uses `zlib::inflate`, and when that fails `zlib::inflate_raw` on the bytes after the 2-byte header, keeping whatever output was produced either way, then applies the predictor from its parms (/Predictor 2 is TIFF, 10 and up PNG, with /Columns default 1, /Colors default 1, /BitsPerComponent default 8); LZWDecode or LZW uses `pf_lzw` with /EarlyChange (default 1) and the same predictor rules; ASCIIHexDecode or AHx, ASCII85Decode or A85, RunLengthDecode or RL use their decoders; Crypt does nothing; any other filter (images such as DCTDecode, JPXDecode, CCITTFaxDecode, JBIG2Decode) stops the chain and returns false with the data decoded so far. Return true.

`pf_text(n int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends a PDF text string as UTF-8: bytes 254 255 start UTF-16 big endian (`dm::dm_utf16`), bytes 239 187 191 start UTF-8, and anything else is PDFDocEncoding: bytes map to their Latin-1 code points except 24 to 31 (0x02D8, 0x02C7, 0x02C6, 0x02D9, 0x02DD, 0x02DB, 0x02DA, 0x02DC), 128 to 160 (0x2022, 0x2020, 0x2021, 0x2026, 0x2014, 0x2013, 0x0192, 0x2044, 0x2039, 0x203A, 0x2212, 0x2030, 0x201E, 0x201C, 0x201D, 0x2018, 0x2019, 0x201A, 0x2122, 0xFB01, 0xFB02, 0x0141, 0x0152, 0x0160, 0x0178, 0x017D, 0x0131, 0x0142, 0x0153, 0x0161, 0x017E, 0xFFFD, 0x20AC). For a name node append its bytes as they are.

`pf_date(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and formats a PDF date "D:YYYYMMDDHHmmSS" (the "D:" prefix optional, any trailing part optional) as "YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:SS" using only the parts present (year alone gives "YYYY"); text that does not start with four digits after the prefix is copied as is.

`pf_setup_crypt()` reads the trailer's /Encrypt (remember its object number in `pf_enc_num` when it is a ref) and, when it is a dict with /Filter /Standard, collects /V, /R, /Length (bits, default 40), /O, /U, /OE, /UE, /P, /EncryptMetadata (default true) and the first element of the trailer's /ID; maps methods: V 1 and 2 use RC4 for streams and strings; V 4 looks up /StmF and /StrF (default /Identity) in /CF, where a filter whose /CFM is /V2 is RC4, /AESV2 is AES-128, /AESV3 AES-256 and /None or /Identity none; V 5 uses AES-256 for both. Keep every number as a plain `int` (never i32). Then call exactly `dcrypt::dc_setup(r, v, length, o, u, oe, ue, p, id0, em, stm, str)`: revision first, then version, key length in bits, the O, U, OE and UE strings, P, the first ID string, EncryptMetadata as a bool, the stream method and the string method. When it returns false add the warning "encrypted with a password; text could not be decrypted". A /Filter other than /Standard adds the warning "encrypted with an unsupported security handler". After a successful setup clear `pf_cache` so objects parse again with decryption.

`pf_collect_pages()` walks the page tree from the catalog's /Pages depth first, in /Kids order, guarding against cycles with a visited set of node indices and stopping at 100000 pages: a node with /Kids is an intermediate node (also when its /Type says Page), other dicts are pages. Inherit /Resources, /MediaBox, /CropBox and /Rotate from ancestors when a page lacks them. Record each page in `pf_pages` with its object number (from the ref that led to it, -1 when inline) and set `pf_pageof` for that number. Normalize /Rotate to 0, 90, 180 or 270. When the tree yields no pages, scan all objects in the xref for dicts with /Type /Page in object number order and use those.

`pf_open(data const byte[:]) bool` resets the state, copies data into `pf_buf` (false when "%PDF" does not appear in its first 1024 bytes), finds the last "startxref" in the final 2048 bytes and reads the int after it; reads the chain with `pf_read_xref`; when that fails or the trailer has no /Root, call `pf_rebuild()`. Sets `pf_root` and `pf_info` from the trailer (`pf_dget`), calls `pf_setup_crypt()`, re-reads them after a successful setup (cache cleared), and calls `pf_collect_pages()`; when there are no pages and the table was not rebuilt, rebuild once and collect again. Returns true when the file was read.

`pf_info_meta()` stores document information with `dm::dm_set_meta`: from `pf_info` the keys Title, Author, Subject, Keywords, Creator and Producer under the lower-case names title, author, subject, keywords, creator and producer (through `pf_text`), and CreationDate and ModDate as created and modified (through `pf_text` then `pf_date`); when no title was found, decode the catalog's /Metadata stream and take the text inside the first rdf:li element after "<dc:title" (with `dm::dm_decode_entities`). Also store "pages" as the page count, and "encrypted" as "yes" when the trailer has /Encrypt.

`pf_dest_page(d int) int` returns the 0-based page index a destination points to, or -1: a name or string is looked up in the catalog's /Dests dict or in the /Names /Dests name tree (walk /Kids and /Names arrays, comparing keys as bytes, at most 64 levels); a dict with /D uses that; an array's first element is a page ref (look up `pf_pageof` of its object number) or an int page index.

`pf_outline(out_heads bool)` walks the catalog's /Outlines tree from /First through /Next and each item's /First depth first (visited set, at most 10000 items, level is the depth from 1, capped at 6) and for every item with a non-empty /Title (through `pf_text`) records its level, its title and the page of its /Dest, or of its /A action's /D when the action's /S is /GoTo; the results go into the module lists `pf_ol_level int[>..]`, `pf_ol_page int[>..]` and `pf_ol_text byte[>..]` with `pf_ol_at int[>..]` and `pf_ol_len int[>..]` (cleared first). When out_heads is true nothing else happens; the text layer uses the lists to mark headings.

`pf_fields()` adds the form fields of the catalog's /AcroForm /Fields to the document model: walk each field and its /Kids depth first (visited set, at most 20000), building the full name by joining the /T values of the field and its ancestors with periods; a field with a /V value adds a `dm::DM_FIELD` block "full name: value", where a string value goes through `pf_text`, a name value is its bytes, an array value is its string or name elements joined with ", ", and an int or real is printed as a number; skip values that are empty or the name Off. Before the first field add a `dm::DM_HEAD` level 2 block "Form fields".

`pf_annots(page int)` adds the comment annotations of page index page as `dm::DM_NOTE` blocks: for each dict in the page's /Annots whose /Subtype is Text, FreeText, Highlight, Underline, StrikeOut, Squiggly, Note, Stamp, Caret, Square, Circle, Line, Polygon, PolyLine or Ink and that has a non-empty /Contents, the note is "Comment" (plus " by " and the /T author when present), ": " and the contents (through `pf_text`).
